    "verdict": "Reported cancellation/worker-lifetime behavior reproduced once on Linux.",
    "observation": "Five completed cancelled Tasks leave five live queue-waiting worker threads and five database FDs at five seconds. Main returns, then the parent timeout terminates the otherwise live process.",
    "attribution": "The generic asyncio graph identifies the five cancelled Tasks and their main_loop creation stacks. An explicit extra Thread.start observer joins each actual worker to its Task, recovering connect_to_db and the batch origin absent from worker physical stacks.",
    "diagnosis_from_source": "CancelledError bypasses _connect except Exception in Python 3.11; __aenter__ never finishes, so __aexit__ does not run. The worker keeps its running flag. This is source-supported interpretation, not an autonomous detector.",
    "repair_guidance_not_tested": "Handle cancellation during connection entry, stop the worker, and close a connector result that completes after cancellation in its owning worker. No patch or alternate version was executed.",
    "limits": [
      "Linux 3.11.6 adapts a Windows 3.11.5 report.",
      "Only a five-second observation establishes retained resources; no claim of infinite lifetime from the snapshot alone.",
      "No cancellation/write locking workload ran; no database corruption claim.",
      "The extra thread producer is required for resource attribution; the generic asyncio collector alone has no thread join.",
      "No new selected coroutine-creation case is claimed from the worker relation."
    ],
